<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Mosque of Kilwa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Mosque of Kilwa, on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilwa-kisiwani">Kilwa Kisiwani</a> off southern Tanzania, is one of the oldest mosques on the East African coast, begun around the 11th century. It was greatly enlarged with domes and vaults in the 14th century by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> al-Hasan ibn Sulaiman, builder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husuni-kubwa">Husuni Kubwa</a>. Its ruins form part of a World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-kilwa.jpg" alt="Great Mosque of Kilwa" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ron Van Oers · CC BY-SA 3.0 igo · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ruins_of_Kilwa_Kisiwani_and_Ruins_of_Songo_Mnara-108279.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The oldest part is the smaller northern prayer hall of the first phase of building. It had 16 bays supported by nine pillars, carved from coral at first and later replaced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, and was entirely roofed; in the 13th century side pilasters and transverse beams were added. Inscriptions give 1269 as the year work began on the minaret. As trade brought Kilwa wealth, the mosque was rebuilt and renovated many times, and between 1294 and 1302 it was extended southward.</p><p>In the early fourteenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> al-Hasan ibn Sulaiman added a southern extension with a great dome. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> described it after visiting the Kilwa Sultanate in 1331, though not accurately: he claimed the mosque was built entirely of wood, while its stone walls are older than the fourteenth century. A large dome at the south-east may have been the sultan's mausoleum. When Kilwa's wealth declined in the late 14th century, building and maintenance fell off and the mosque was reported to be in ruins.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The mosque stands at the top of a small hill and is wider than it is long. Its walls are of squared and coursed coral stone, with chips set between the courses to level them, laid first in mud mortar and later in lime mortar, and coral columns held up a high vaulted ceiling. It is one of the first mosques built without a sahn, or courtyard: the southern extension is made of square bays with no central court. A separate domed chamber served the sultan for private prayer, and the mihrab has a broken arch resting on two columns with rectangular capitals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Coins in the name of 'Ali bin al-Hasan, the strongest evidence for identifying an early ruler of Kilwa, were found in pots inside the walls and on the upper two levels, which suggests the mosque may be older than his reign. In the late eighteenth century a new mihrab was knocked through the southern wall of the extension and the roof fell in, while the northern part was given up and never rebuilt. The ruins lie within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilwa-kisiwani">Kilwa Kisiwani</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> and a National Historic Site of Tanzania. Between 2005 and 2009 the Zamani Project recorded them with terrestrial 3D laser scanning, together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husuni-kubwa">Husuni Kubwa</a>, the Gereza and the Malindi Mosque.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Great Mosque of Kilwa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Great Mosque of Kilwa</b>, whose oldest part dates from the 11th or 12th century, was enlarged in the 13th or early 14th century with a domed extension; it is the oldest standing mosque on the East African coast, and its great dome remained the largest in East…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kilwa-sultanate">Kilwa Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
